WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.460
<v Instructor>Now let's get into S3 security.</v>

2
00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:05.400
And first of all, we're going to look at S3 encryption.

3
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.460
And it's good to know, first of all,

4
00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:11.880
SSE-S3 which allows you to encrypt S3 objects

5
00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:15.330
using keys handled and managed by AWS.

6
00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:17.280
Then we have SSE-KMS,

7
00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:19.230
where you leverage the KMS service

8
00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:21.390
to manage your encryption keys.

9
00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:22.320
Why would you do this?

10
00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:26.220
Well, first of all, any API call that is done

11
00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:29.550
through SSE-KMS will appear as an API call in KMS,

12
00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:32.610
and therefore, your key usage appears in CloudTrail,

13
00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:35.220
which is very helpful for audit purposes.

14
00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.830
The second thing is that it's a very good security

15
00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:40.500
in case your bucket is made public,

16
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:42.810
because even if an bucket is made public,

17
00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:44.670
or an object is made public,

18
00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:47.790
and that object is encrypted with SSE-KMS,

19
00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:49.260
then it can never be read

20
00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:52.410
because a public anonymous user cannot have access

21
00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:54.150
to your KMS key.

22
00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:57.060
Finally, if you do upload objects

23
00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:59.760
with the SSE-KMS encryption scheme,

24
00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:01.490
you need to make sure that you have access

25
00:01:01.490 --> 00:01:04.890
to the IAM permission, kms:GenerateDataKey,

26
00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:08.070
because this is how the data key is being used

27
00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:10.950
to upload that object specifically.

28
00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:12.480
You also have SSE-C.

29
00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:13.470
So, in that example,

30
00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:15.930
we want to manage our own encryption keys,

31
00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:18.060
but we pass them on to Amazon S3,

32
00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:20.580
and the encryption is done server side.

33
00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:23.190
Or, if you prefer to manage your own encryption keys

34
00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:25.620
and also do the encryption client side,

35
00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:27.030
then you use client-side encryption,

36
00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:29.280
and therefore you encrypt

37
00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:31.980
and your decrypt your data client side.

38
00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:33.720
Finally, if you use Glacier,

39
00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:38.520
all the data is going to be by default AES-256 encrypted,

40
00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:42.240
and the key will remain under the control of AWS.

41
00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.730
Now, you also have encryption in transit,

42
00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:47.640
and in that case Amazon S3 will expose two endpoints.

43
00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.670
You'll have the HTTP endpoint, which is non-encrypted,

44
00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:52.857
and that's not the default one your client use,

45
00:01:52.857 --> 00:01:54.840
and the default one used by your client

46
00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:57.780
is the HTTPS endpoint to guarantee that you have encryption

47
00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:01.560
in flight using SSL or TLS certificates.

48
00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:03.120
So you can use whatever endpoint you want,

49
00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:05.730
but of course HTTPS is recommended,

50
00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:07.710
and it is actually mandatory if you want

51
00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:11.070
to use the SSE-C encryption scheme.

52
00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:15.240
Finally, if you wanted to force your users to use HTTPS,

53
00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:17.580
then you will use a bucket policy,

54
00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:18.780
and you will use the condition

55
00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:22.323
on the aws:SecureTransport condition.

56
00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:25.530
And we're good to go with encryption in flights.

57
00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:27.900
Next, we have events in S3 buckets,

58
00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:29.190
and so there are multiple ones.

59
00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:30.960
The first one is S3 Access Logs,

60
00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:32.430
which will give you detailed records

61
00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:35.370
of all the requests that are made into a buckets,

62
00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:36.780
and it might take hours to deliver

63
00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:39.150
and it might be delivered to another S3 buckets,

64
00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:40.350
and it might be incomplete.

65
00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:42.035
This is from the documentation, this is best effort,

66
00:02:42.035 --> 00:02:44.943
but still a really good access log to get into S3.

67
00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:47.700
S3 Event Notifications now,

68
00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:48.990
which is to retrieve notifications

69
00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:51.810
whenever certain events happen in your buckets.

70
00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:53.700
For example, new objects are being created,

71
00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:54.990
objects are being removed,

72
00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:56.010
objects are being restored,

73
00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:58.590
or there is a replication event happening,

74
00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:00.690
and the destinations of these S3 events

75
00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:04.650
can be SNS, SQS queue and Lambda.

76
00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:08.490
And so, typically, these S3 event notifications are going

77
00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:10.380
to be delivered within seconds,

78
00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:13.200
but it can sometime take a minute or two,

79
00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:17.130
and you will receive a notification for every single object

80
00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:20.310
if you have enabled versioning on your S3 bucket.

81
00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:22.920
But if somehow you haven't enabled versioning,

82
00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:26.130
and in the very rare occasion that you're doing two writes

83
00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:28.650
at the same time on the same object,

84
00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:32.070
then you may receive just one notification and not two,

85
00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:34.050
and that is outlined in the documentation,

86
00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:36.210
but, for the rest, you will receive a notification

87
00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:40.770
for every single object inserted into your S3 buckets.

88
00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:43.800
Now, for Trusted Advisor, check the bucket permissions.

89
00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:45.180
So this will be helpful, for example,

90
00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:47.790
to know if your bucket is being public.

91
00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:50.160
And you have Amazon EventBridge where you need

92
00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:53.550
to enable CloudTrail object-level logging on S3 first

93
00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:56.160
and then the target of Amazon EventBridge

94
00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:00.483
could be Lambda functions, SQS, SNS, etc, etc.

95
00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:03.840
Next, S3 security, how do you secure a bucket?

96
00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:05.370
Well, first you have user-based security

97
00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:06.780
where you have IAM policies,

98
00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:09.180
and by setting up an IAM policy on one of your user,

99
00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:12.690
you allow it to access a specific bucket.

100
00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:14.250
And you have resource-based policy,

101
00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:15.720
which is bucket policies,

102
00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:18.150
so this is bucket-wide rules for the S3 console,

103
00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:19.470
and we've seen that it's very helpful

104
00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:21.300
when we have cross-account access,

105
00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:22.740
because it allows the accounts

106
00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:25.080
who wants to access the S3 buckets not to assume a role

107
00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:26.790
and give up its permissions.

108
00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:28.950
Then we have object ACL finer grain,

109
00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:31.140
and finally bucket access control list,

110
00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:32.250
which is less common,

111
00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:35.250
and the exam probably will not talk to you about the ACLs,

112
00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:37.350
only talk about the S3 bucket policies

113
00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:40.890
because this is the way forward for the exam.

114
00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:43.200
Okay, now let's talk about the S3 bucket policies

115
00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:44.370
in details.

116
00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:46.050
So you should use an S3 bucket policy

117
00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:48.450
in order to grant public access to the bucket,

118
00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:50.970
or force objects to be encrypted at upload,

119
00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:54.120
or grant access to another account, cross-account access,

120
00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:56.640
and you can get conditions on many different things,

121
00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:58.650
and this is very important to remember those.

122
00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:00.780
The first one is SourceIp.

123
00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:03.060
So this allows you to have a condition

124
00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:05.700
on the public or elastic IP.

125
00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:08.820
And we have a second one called VpcSourceIp,

126
00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:11.820
which allows you to get a condition on the private IP,

127
00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:15.123
but only if you are using a VPC endpoint.

128
00:05:15.990 --> 00:05:17.430
But there is another option.

129
00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:20.400
It's called Source VPC or Source VPC Endpoint.

130
00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:22.260
And this is when you define a VPC endpoint,

131
00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:25.080
we saw this in the next slide or the one after,

132
00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:28.230
and this is very helpful when you have a private access

133
00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:30.180
to your S3 buckets and you want to say,

134
00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:33.270
okay, only IP coming from this VPC endpoint

135
00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:34.950
or this VPC are allowed.

136
00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:36.180
But again, this is not by IP,

137
00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:39.060
this is by VPC or VPC endpoint.

138
00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:41.550
Okay. Then we have CloudFront Origin Identity.

139
00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:43.350
So it's possible to have a condition

140
00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:45.810
on saying only a CloudFront distribution

141
00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:47.850
with an origin identity is able

142
00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:50.550
to access my S3 bucket policy.

143
00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:51.810
And finally, you can have conditions

144
00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:53.820
on multifactor authentication.

145
00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:56.400
If you wanna see many different S3 bucket policies,

146
00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:58.680
please use this link and have a look,

147
00:05:58.680 --> 00:05:59.790
learn a little bit more,

148
00:05:59.790 --> 00:06:02.460
if these are not very familiar for you.

149
00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:04.620
So now pre-signed URLs, what are those?

150
00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:07.680
You can generate pre-signed URLs using the SDK or the CLI.

151
00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:12.030
And this for downloads or for uploads, they are both valid.

152
00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:14.220
And the idea is that you sign these URLs

153
00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:16.560
with your IAM credentials.

154
00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:18.180
And so whoever has this pre-signed URL

155
00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:21.000
has exact same right that you were given

156
00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:23.550
when you created that pre-signed URL.

157
00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:26.100
It's valid for a default of one hour,

158
00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:27.300
but you can change the timeout

159
00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:30.090
with the expires-in arguments,

160
00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:32.940
and users will inherit the permission of the person

161
00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:36.360
who created or generated the URL for GET or PUT,

162
00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:38.910
GET is for download and PUT is for upload.

163
00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:41.310
So when we do use pre-signed URLs?

164
00:06:41.310 --> 00:06:42.420
We will use them, for example,

165
00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:44.010
if we want to allow logged-in users

166
00:06:44.010 --> 00:06:47.220
to only download a premium video on your S3 buckets

167
00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:50.340
on demand, or allow an ever-changing list of users

168
00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:53.610
to download files by generating URLs dynamically.

169
00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:54.990
Also, if you want to allow a user

170
00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:57.960
to upload a file temporarily to a very precise location

171
00:06:57.960 --> 00:06:58.793
in your bucket,

172
00:06:58.793 --> 00:07:02.100
and then make sure they don't have any rights after this.

173
00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:06.480
Okay, so now let's talk about VPC Endpoint Gateway for S3.

174
00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:08.790
So let's say you have a VPC and, in it,

175
00:07:08.790 --> 00:07:11.250
you have an EC2 instance, it's in the public subnet,

176
00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:13.560
and it wants to access an S3 bucket.

177
00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:16.110
Now the S3 bucket has a public access

178
00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:19.710
and, therefore, you need to go through an internet gateway

179
00:07:19.710 --> 00:07:22.050
for the EC2 instance to get a public IP,

180
00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:25.230
go through the internet gateway and then get access

181
00:07:25.230 --> 00:07:27.900
through the public internet to the S3 bucket.

182
00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:30.150
Now, the S3 bucket can have a bucket policy,

183
00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:33.480
and we can filter, have a condition for the bucket policy

184
00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:37.560
by using AWS:SourceIP, and this must be a public IP

185
00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:40.320
or an elastic IP, which are public by the way.

186
00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:41.550
That's option one.

187
00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:46.140
Option two is we have a instance in a private subnet,

188
00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:48.960
and we want to access the S3 bucket policy.

189
00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:50.220
Now, there's a way where we could have

190
00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:53.130
this private instance go through a net gateway

191
00:07:53.130 --> 00:07:56.010
in this public subnet, go through the internet gateway

192
00:07:56.010 --> 00:07:57.270
and then go through the public route.

193
00:07:57.270 --> 00:08:00.210
So you go shoop like this up and then right,

194
00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:02.040
but there is a better way, right?

195
00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:04.590
Because here you go through the public internet,

196
00:08:04.590 --> 00:08:06.870
and you access your bucket publicly.

197
00:08:06.870 --> 00:08:11.250
Another way to do this is to use a VPC Endpoint Gateway.

198
00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:13.200
And you define this VPC Endpoint Gateway,

199
00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:17.040
and then all the traffic remains on the AWS network.

200
00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:18.720
It stays private.

201
00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:20.580
And now the idea is that you can access your S3 bucket

202
00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:22.920
directly through this VPC endpoint,

203
00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:25.080
and if you wanted to use an S3 bucket policy,

204
00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:26.670
you could have two conditions.

205
00:08:26.670 --> 00:08:29.850
The first one is AWS:SourceVpce,

206
00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:32.490
where you can specify one or few VPC endpoints

207
00:08:32.490 --> 00:08:35.310
that is allowed to go through to this buckets,

208
00:08:35.310 --> 00:08:40.310
or AWS:SourceVpc to encompass all the possible VPC endpoints

209
00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:43.110
that belong to a specific VPC.

210
00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:45.900
And so these will be the two very important conditions

211
00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:47.910
to remember for your S3 buckets.

212
00:08:47.910 --> 00:08:50.550
Number one is SourceIP, which is for public IP,

213
00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:53.280
and the second one is SourceVpc or SourceVpce,

214
00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:57.090
when you have a VPC endpoint to access your S3 buckets.

215
00:08:57.090 --> 00:08:57.930
I hope that makes sense

216
00:08:57.930 --> 00:09:00.540
and I hope this diagram is something that sheds a lot

217
00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:02.460
of lights on these S3 bucket policies

218
00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:04.020
and networking conditions.

219
00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:06.960
Okay, finally, for S3 security.

220
00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:09.930
So let's talk about Object Locks and Glacier Vault Lock.

221
00:09:09.930 --> 00:09:11.340
So they're very similar.

222
00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:14.220
S3 Object Lock allows you to adopt a WORM,

223
00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:17.310
so write once, read many model,

224
00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:19.470
which is to block an object version deletion

225
00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:20.790
for a specified amount of time.

226
00:09:20.790 --> 00:09:22.980
So you have the certainty that will never, ever,

227
00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:24.360
ever be deleted.

228
00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:26.490
It's the same for Glacier Vault Lock,

229
00:09:26.490 --> 00:09:28.350
where you adopt the same model,

230
00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:30.480
and you lock the policy for future edits,

231
00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:31.740
so that it can no longer be changed

232
00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:35.340
and any object put into Glacier will never ever be deleted,

233
00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:37.260
which is extremely helpful if you have compliance

234
00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:39.270
and data retention requirements,

235
00:09:39.270 --> 00:09:41.250
and you need to prove these to auditors,

236
00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:42.690
then it's a great way to show that,

237
00:09:42.690 --> 00:09:45.390
because you've set up a Object Lock or a Glacier Vault Lock,

238
00:09:45.390 --> 00:09:48.000
then the object will never ever be deleted.

239
00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:51.840
So very simple, take an object, put it in the vault

240
00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:52.830
and lock that vault,

241
00:09:52.830 --> 00:09:55.500
and the object can never ever be deleted.

242
00:09:55.500 --> 00:09:58.020
So that rounds up everything for S3 security.

243
00:09:58.020 --> 00:10:00.510
So there should be a lot of things that you already knew,

244
00:10:00.510 --> 00:10:03.000
but probably one or two new things in there.

245
00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:04.860
And remember, going into the exam,

246
00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:07.140
the very important things is all about the details.

247
00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:09.060
So everything I'm saying about the details is

248
00:10:09.060 --> 00:10:10.800
what you should be paying attention on.

249
00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:12.240
Alright, so that's it for this lecture.

250
00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:13.073
I hope you liked it,

251
00:10:13.073 --> 00:10:14.850
and I will see you in the next lecture.

